# Engineering standards

Apply when **writing** code in this repo. Authoring principles below are adapted from [google/eng-practices](https://github.com/google/eng-practices) ([repo summary](docs/references/google-eng-practices.md)). For the full **review** checklist, use that doc in **sql-gym-pre-review**—not here.

## Writing changes

- **Minimal scope:** Smallest correct change; no drive-by refactors. Prefer **small PRs**—one main concern each when practical.
- **Design fit:** Put change in the right layer; integrate with existing design. Do not add abstraction or features for a hypothetical future.
- **Simplicity:** Straightforward solution first; avoid extra layers readers cannot follow quickly.
- **DRY:** Reuse existing helpers when they fit. Extract only when duplication is real and the abstraction is obvious.
- **Naming:** Names should communicate purpose without reading surrounding comments.
- **Conventions:** Match the codebase. Repo linter/config and local patterns beat generic style when they conflict.
- **Style vs behavior:** Do not mix large formatting-only edits with functional changes in one PR.
- **PR descriptions:** *What*, *why*, and how tested—not only files touched.

## Tests, comments, documentation

- **Tests:** Add with production code when possible. Tests should fail when behavior breaks; keep them simple—no clever test code.
- **Functionality:** Exercise edge cases you can; align behavior with the PRD / Linear acceptance criteria.
- **Comments:** Explain *why*, not *what*. If the code needs a long *what* comment, simplify the code. Remove obsolete TODOs.
- **Documentation:** Update READMEs or module docs when setup, behavior, or usage changes.

## When reviewing

Use [docs/references/google-eng-practices.md](docs/references/google-eng-practices.md) and **sql-gym-pre-review**—do not duplicate the reviewer checklist in this rule.

**Python:** [Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html); Ruff/format config overrides when present.

**SQL:** [Simon Holywell SQL Style Guide](https://www.sqlstyle.guide/) for portable ANSI clarity; repo SQL linter/dialect overrides when configured.

**HTML/CSS / JS (only when the PRD includes a web UI):** [HTML/CSS guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/htmlcssguide.html), [JavaScript guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/jsguide.html) for framework code; frontend linter config overrides when present.
